## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design augmented self-balancing BSTs for dynamic range queries—a common requirement in analytics and data processing applications.

## Key Concepts

- **AVL balancing**: Maintaining height balance factor (|left.height - right.height| ≤ 1) through rotations
- **Augmented BST data**: Storing additional aggregate information (cnt, sum) at each node
- **Range query on BST**: Using the BST property to efficiently prune irrelevant subtrees
- **Correctness and complexity analysis**: Ensuring O(log n) operations and verifying invariants

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.cnt = 1;
    this.height = 1;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.sum = key;
  }
}

function getHeight(node) { return node ? node.height : 0; }
function getSum(node) { return node ? node.sum : 0; }
function update(node) {
  node.height = 1 + Math.max(getHeight(node.left), getHeight(node.right));
  node.sum = getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + getSum(node.right);
}

function rightRotate(y) {
  const x = y.left;
  y.left = x.right;
  x.right = y;
  update(y);
  update(x);
  return x;
}

function rangeSum(node, a, b) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  if (node.key < a) return rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
  if (node.key > b) return rangeSum(node.left, a, b);
  return getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
}
```

